# Promotion protocol

## Branch flow

`feat|fix|chore|docs|test/*` → **PR** → `develop` → **PR** → `preprod` → **PR** (+ production approval) → `main`

Never `git merge` protected branches locally for the purpose of shipping; use GitHub PRs so CI and branch protection apply.

## PR titles (conventional)

- `promote: develop -> preprod (rc-YYYY.MM.DD)`
- `release: preprod -> main (vX.Y.Z)`

## GitHub CLI examples

Replace `OWNER/REPO` with the repo you are promoting (e.g. `heirlabs/heir-back`).

```bash
# After your feature merged to develop — promote develop into preprod
gh pr create --repo OWNER/REPO --base preprod --head develop \
  --title "promote: develop -> preprod ($(date +%Y.%m.%d))" \
  --body-file .github/pull_request_template/promotion.md

# After preprod soak — release to main (needs GitHub Environment "production" approval)
gh pr create --repo OWNER/REPO --base main --head preprod \
  --title "release: preprod -> main (vX.Y.Z)" \
  --body-file .github/pull_request_template/promotion.md
```

## Refusals

- Do **not** run `gh workflow run deploy-prod.yml` (or any deploy workflow) to ship; merges to protected branches trigger deploy workflows in each **service** repo.
- Do **not** `git push origin main` / `preprod` / `develop`.

If the user insists on bypassing the pipeline, refuse unless they document an explicit emergency with accountable owner (see `10-branching.mdc` override).
